#!/usr/bin/env perl
# lmer() against lme4::lmer() and lmerTest's Satterthwaite degrees of freedom.
#
# PROVENANCE
#
# %EXPECT is frozen output of t/lmer.R.R under R 4.6.1 with lme4 2.0-6 and
# lmerTest 3.2-1 (re-run with `Rscript t/lmer.R.R`; it also writes
# t/sleepstudy.csv, t/Penicillin.csv, t/Pastes.csv and t/lme*.csv).  The
# models are lme4's and lmerTest's documented examples and statsmodels':
#
#   sleep_*      lme4 man/sleepstudy.Rd and lmer.Rd, lmerTest
#                tests/test_summary.R: Reaction ~ Days with a correlated
#                random intercept and slope by Subject (REML and ML), the
#                same uncorrelated, and the intercept alone.
#   penicillin   lme4 man/Penicillin.Rd: crossed plate and sample.
#   pastes       lme4 man/Pastes.Rd: cask nested in batch, (1 | batch/cask).
#   lme*_*       statsmodels 0.14.6 regression/tests/test_lme.py: its
#                results/lme<k>.csv corpora (100 groups of 1 to 5), with
#                dependent ("drf") and independent ("irf") random effects, by
#                ML and REML -- the fits whose lme4 output its
#                results/lme_r_results.py pins.
#
# Each record holds lme4's default fit (pkg_*) and a tightly converged one --
# lme4's own criterion, minimised to nlminb()'s tightest tolerance and then
# by Nelder-Mead -- with lmerTest's degrees of freedom on it.
#
# TOLERANCE
#
# The REML or ML criterion is flat enough at its minimum that theta is only
# determined to about sqrt(machine epsilon) of its size: two minimisers of the
# same function, lme4's tightest and this module's, stop 8e-8 apart in theta
# on sleepstudy while their criterion values agree to 1e-15.  So:
#
#   criterion, log-likelihood       1e-12 relative (observed 9.1e-15)
#   fixed effects                   1e-6 of their standard error (observed
#                                   7.1e-8)
#   theta, standard errors, sigma,
#     variance components, t        1e-5 relative (observed 1.4e-6)
#   Satterthwaite df                1e-4 relative (observed 1.8e-6; lmerTest
#                                   takes its Hessian by numDeriv, this
#                                   module by Richardson differences of its
#                                   own)
#   p-values                        1e-4 on the log scale
#
# Against lme4's default fit, which stops short of the optimum, 1e-4 relative
# on standard errors and 1e-3 on theta (observed 3.9e-4, on lme10's ML fit),
# and neither where lme4's default stopped at a worse local optimum, which it
# does on lme07's ML fit.  A variance component on its boundary is compared
# absolutely, to 1e-5 of the residual sd.  The observed worst of each class is
# printed at the end.

